<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bukka Raya I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1377">1377</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Bukka I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangama-dynasty">Sangama dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bukka Raya I was a co-founder and ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, reigning from about 1356 to 1377 after his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara-i">Harihara I</a>. A member of the Sangama family, he extended the kingdom across the south, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, and through his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumara-kampana">Kumara Kampana</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a>. He also built up the capital, Vijayanagara, on the Tungabhadra river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bukka-raya-i.jpg" alt="Bukka Raya I" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bukkaraya_I_Coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bukka was a son of Bhavana Sangama. Little is known of his early life or that of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a>, also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara-i">Harihara I</a>, and accounts rest on competing theories. One, put forward by Father Heras, stresses the brothers' devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> deities such as Virupaksha and Keshava, and notes that they signed &quot;Sri Virupaksha&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> letters even in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Telugu and Tamil records; Desai cites Ferishta's name for the rulers, &quot;Roise of Carnatic&quot;, as a sign of their Karnataka origin.</p><p>A second theory makes the brothers commanders in the army of the Kakatiya king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a> defeated Warangal they were taken as prisoners to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, where they converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>; they later escaped, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> under the influence of the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidyaranya">Vidyaranya</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>During Bukka's 21-year reign (37 years, according to Nuniz) the kingdom prospered and expanded. He defeated the Shambuvaraya kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> and the Reddis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondavidu">Kondavidu</a> by 1360 and annexed the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penukonda">Penukonda</a>. In 1371 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Sultanate of Madurai</a> and extended his territory south as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumara-kampana">Kumara Kampana</a> campaigned with him, and their deeds are recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> work Madura Vijayam, also called Veerakamparaya Charitram, written by Gangambika.</p><p>By 1374 Bukka had the upper hand over the Bahmanis in the Tungabhadra-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> doab and had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> and part of the kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorins</a> of Malabar paid him tribute. He clashed with the Bahmani sultans Mohammed Shah I and Mujahid Shah, and he is said to have sent a mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>Under Bukka the capital settled at Vijayanagara, on the south side of the river, which was more secure than the earlier capital at Anegondi. He made the Telugu poet Nachana Soma his court poet, and dozens of scholars worked under the guidance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidyaranya">Vidyaranya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayana">Sayana</a>; a group headed by Sayana produced commentaries on the Samhitas of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and on several of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a> and Aranyakas. Bukka was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara-ii">Harihara II</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bukka Raya I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shortly afterwards, as Tughluq authority in the south crumbled, the brothers Harihara I and <b>Bukka I</b> of the Sangama family established their own kingdom on the Tungabhadra, traditionally in 1336, with its capital at the city they named Vijayanagara, &quot;city of victory&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rising Vijayanagara Empire attacked from the north; Kumara Kampana, son of <b>Bukka I</b>, defeated the sultan around 1370, and the sultanate's last remnants disappeared by 1378.</blockquote><a href="/state/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
